                        HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ



   Abuela didn’t tell her. The old woman laughed anyway, one short
bark, and went back to her goat.
   Eliseo found me by the cooler. He had taken off the cape and folded
it over his arm like a waiter’s towel, and he held out a phone with a
screen so cracked it looked like a map of the river.
   “Tu número.”
   “For what?”
   “Semana Santa. Por si vuelve el parlante.” In case the speaker comes
back. He watched me type it in, took the phone back, and added my
name with one thumb. I leaned over to look. He’d saved me as JC EL
DEL CABLE.
   “That’s not my name.”
   “Ya lo es,” said Eliseo.
   It is now.
   When Wilfredo’s cab pulled out of the batey, the woman from the
colmado was standing in her doorway with her arms folded. The white
box on its bracket over her head was still dark. The plug lay on the
counter where I’d left it, and she was taking great care not to look at it.



Thursday, she set the bundle on the table.
   It was morning, with the shutters half-closed against the heat and
the speaker over the colmado across the street murmuring away,
pressed place and all. Abuela didn’t mention it. She unwrapped the
Madre layer by layer, set her in the middle of the table, and looked at
me.
   “Ahora sí,” she said.
   Now, yes.
   I laid two fingers on the stone.
   It was warm, and it was heavier than it looked, and the coin in my
pocket went off like an alarm. The room opened.
   It wasn’t the way it had been at the wall, a memory of mine with the
edges blurred. This was somebody else’s. Dark and close, pressed
against a heartbeat that wasn’t mine, with cloth wrapped around me,




